-When there is a need to disable the Linter for part of a file, ranged ignores can be used. The syntax for a ranged ignore
-is `<!-- linter-disable -->` or `%%linter-disable%%` with an optional `<!-- linter-enable -->` or `%%linter-disable%%` where you want the Linter to start back up with its linting.
-Leaving off the ending of a range ignore will assume you want to ignore the file contents from the start of the range ignore to the end of the file. So be careful when not ending a range ignore.
+When there is a need to disable the Linter for part of a file, ranged ignores can be used. Put a marker on its own line using either `<!-- linter-disable -->` / `<!-- linter-enable -->` or `%% linter-disable %%` / `%% linter-enable %%`. Leaving off the ending marker ignores from the start marker to the end of the file.
+
+Add a comma-separated list of rule aliases to affect only those rules, for example `<!-- linter-disable capitalize-headings, trailing-spaces -->`. Rule aliases are case-insensitive. You can also use `linter-disable-next-line` or `linter-disable-next-n-lines: N` for a line-scoped ignore. Markers in frontmatter, code, inline code, or math blocks are ignored.

 !!! warning
     Ranged ignores only prevent the values in the ranged ignore from being linted. It *does not* prevent whitespace or other additions around the ranged ignore.
